nd /tmp/srv23.log test-server log files that were not cleaned up alongside the killed processes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9b725038c85341d3","cmd-06fa3e434ae84f4e","cmd-082d307a547c4676","cmd-9a1c8c0aa812427a","cmd-19148a9385424be3","cmd-3135d5830db74e0d","cmd-10589e4e32154273","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes of the task are satisfied with direct, functional evidence: node2/node3 reach each other on TCP 8080, node1's nft forward-chain rejects TCP 23 between them with a TCP reset, and other forwarded traffic (ICMP) passes untouched via the default-accept policy — all verified both before and after restarting every node. The independent provider-wide restart-evidence protocol was also fully and correctly executed for all three nodes, each showing a healthy baseline, a restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity consistent with the fixed lifecycle snapshots, and full functional recovery, including persistence of the nft ACL and forwarding configuration across node1's more complex SONiC reboot. Hygiene is generally good; the only notable issues are a self-corrected nft rule-duplication mistake, an under-justified docker0 address/interface mutation on node1, and small leftover log files from test HTTP servers on node3.","limitations":["The 'other traffic untouched' outcome was only directly tested with ICMP; no other protocols/ports besides 8080 and 23 were exercised end-to-end, though this is reasonable given the accept-by-default policy shown in the ruleset.","Whether removing docker0's address and disabling interfaces-config.service on node1 was strictly necessary for the routing/ACL outcome versus optional cleanup could not be fully determined from the trace."]},"ttft_ms":35460,"type":"result","duration_ms":86125,"uuid":"329fb9bb-79b5-4872-9fab-8e7c7e9237c9","ttft_stream_ms":2433,"time_to_request_ms":36,"first_content_frame_ms":2802,"queued_turn_count":0}
